<!-- ============================================================
     Oracle E-Business Suite Payments File Transmission Privilege Escalation
     CVE-2026-46817 (CWE-269: Improper Privilege Management
                   / CWE-287: Improper Authentication
                   / CWE-306: Missing Authentication for Critical Function)
     Affected: Oracle E-Business Suite 12.2.3 through 12.2.15
               Oracle Payments File Transmission component (ibytransmit)
     MITRE ATT&CK:
       T1190 - Exploit Public-Facing Application (TA0001 Initial Access)
       T1548 - Abuse Elevation Control Mechanism (TA0004 Privilege Escalation)
       T1083 - File and Directory Discovery (TA0007 Discovery)
       T1552.001 - Unsecured Credentials: Credentials in Files (TA0006)
     CISA KEV: Added 2026-07-15, Federal Deadline 2026-07-18
     Rule IDs: 103965-103971
     Author: SecOps Engineer (Purple Team)
     Date: 2026-07-15

     Vulnerability Summary:

     CVE-2026-46817 is a critical unauthenticated privilege escalation
     vulnerability (CVSS 9.8) in the Oracle Payments File Transmission
     component of Oracle E-Business Suite (EBS). The vulnerable endpoint
     /OA_HTML/ibytransmit fails to enforce authentication before processing
     incoming requests. An unauthenticated attacker with HTTP network access
     can send a crafted POST request to this endpoint that invokes an internal
     Oracle Java function and redirects it to read arbitrary files from the
     EBS server filesystem.

     First in-the-wild exploitation was observed on June 27, 2026, approximately
     six weeks after Oracle published patches in the May 2026 Critical Patch
     Update. CISA added CVE-2026-46817 to the Known Exploited Vulnerabilities
     catalog on July 15, 2026.

     Attack chain:
       1. Attacker sends HTTP POST to /OA_HTML/ibytransmit with no credentials.
       2. The missing authentication check allows the request to be processed.
       3. A crafted payload in the POST body invokes an internal Oracle Java
          function (in the Oracle Payments/iPayments namespace).
       4. The function is redirected to read an arbitrary file from the server
          (confirmed: /etc/passwd readable without authentication).
       5. High-value follow-on targets include Oracle wallet files, DBC
          configuration files (containing DB credentials), encryption keys,
          and application configuration files that would enable lateral movement
          to the Oracle Database.

     Detection Approach:

     HTTP / Traefik reverse-proxy layer (rules 103965-103970):
       Rule 103965 (level 7): Base anchor - any HTTP request to the ibytransmit
         endpoint. Used for correlation; all HTTP-layer child rules chain from here.
       Rule 103966 (level 11): POST method to ibytransmit - the attack vector.
         Normal file transmission clients may use GET; unauthenticated POST is
         the exploit delivery method.
       Rule 103967 (level 13): POST to ibytransmit returning HTTP 2xx - auth
         bypass confirmed at the HTTP layer; the server accepted and processed the
         unauthenticated request.
       Rule 103968 (level 14): Request to ibytransmit with file path or LFI
         patterns in the raw request body - attacker payload targeting credential
         files, Oracle DBC/wallet files, or path traversal sequences.
       Rule 103969 (level 15): POST 2xx to ibytransmit with file path payload -
         highest-confidence signal; both the auth bypass and the file read payload
         are present in a single request; confirmed active exploitation.
       Rule 103970 (level 9): Rapid probe burst from single source to ibytransmit
         - automated exploit scanner fingerprint; 8+ requests in 60 seconds.

     Application server access log layer (rule 103971):
       Rule 103971 (level 12): Apache/Oracle HTTP Server combined log format -
         covers environments where EBS traffic is not proxied through Traefik.
         Matches POST to ibytransmit with 2xx response in standard Apache/OHS
         access log format as decoded from syslog or Wazuh agent file ingestion.

     Log source prerequisites:
       Traefik layer: Traefik access logs in JSON format forwarded to Wazuh.
         Field names: RequestMethod, RequestPath, RequestHost, DownstreamStatus,
         ClientHost, rawRequest.
       Oracle HTTP Server / Apache layer: OHS or Apache combined access log
         format forwarded to Wazuh via agent or syslog. Standard fields
         including request method, path, and status code in the log line.

     Severity tiers:
        7 - HTTP request to ibytransmit (any method; info anchor only)
       11 - POST to ibytransmit (attack method; no response code required)
       13 - POST to ibytransmit returning 2xx (auth bypass at HTTP layer)
       14 - Request to ibytransmit with LFI/file path payload present
       15 - POST 2xx to ibytransmit with file path payload (confirmed exploit)
        9 - Probe burst: 8+ ibytransmit requests from same IP in 60 seconds
       12 - Apache/OHS access log: POST to ibytransmit returning 2xx

     False positive guidance:
       103965: Legitimate Oracle Payments File Transmission client software
         may poll ibytransmit on a schedule; baseline known-good client IPs
         and suppress via overrides. Any external/internet-sourced request
         to this endpoint has no legitimate business justification.
       103966: POST to ibytransmit from a legitimate Oracle Payments trading
         partner integration is plausible; review ClientHost against the
         authorized partner IP allowlist.
       103967: A 2xx from ibytransmit to an internal known-good partner IP
         during a scheduled transmission window is expected behavior; flag
         all requests from unknown or external IPs.
       103968: Oracle DBC/wallet file path patterns in a request body have
         virtually no benign explanation from an external caller; treat as
         high-confidence exploitation indicator.
       103969: No benign justification for an unauthenticated POST 2xx
         to ibytransmit containing credential file path patterns; treat as
         confirmed exploitation requiring immediate incident response.
       103970: Legitimate Oracle Payments integration tools do not send
         burst traffic to ibytransmit; bursts from a single IP indicate
         automated scanning or exploit tool activity.
       103971: Authorized Oracle Payments partner transmissions arrive on
         a known schedule from known source IPs; unexpected source IPs or
         off-schedule POSTs returning 2xx indicate active exploitation.
     ============================================================ -->

<group name="privilege_escalation,exploit_pubfacing,oracle_ebs,oracle_payments,cisa_kev,cve_2026_46817,">

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Layer 1: HTTP / Traefik reverse-proxy (JSON access logs)
       Rule IDs: 103965-103970

       CVE-2026-46817 is exploited by sending a crafted POST request
       to /OA_HTML/ibytransmit with no authentication credentials.
       Monitoring the reverse-proxy layer catches exploit delivery
       before any host-level or application-level events fire.
       =========================================================== -->

  <!-- Base anchor: any HTTP request to the Oracle Payments ibytransmit endpoint.

       /OA_HTML/ibytransmit is the Oracle iPayments File Transmission servlet
       (iby = Oracle internal payments namespace). Under normal operation this
       endpoint accepts authenticated file transmission requests from configured
       Oracle Payments trading partner integrations. Any unexpected request to
       this endpoint, especially from external or unrecognized sources, warrants
       investigation.

       This anchor is level 7 (informational correlation only). All HTTP-layer
       child rules (103966-103970) chain from this via if_sid or if_matched_sid
       and raise severity when additional exploitation signals are present. -->
  <rule id="103965" level="7">
    <decoded_as>json</decoded_as>
    <field name="RequestPath" type="pcre2">(?i)/OA_HTML/ibytransmit</field>
    <description>Oracle EBS CVE-2026-46817: HTTP request to Oracle Payments File Transmission endpoint $(RequestPath) from $(ClientHost) at $(RequestHost) -- CVE-2026-46817 attack surface anchor; correlate with child rules 103966-103970 for exploitation signals (T1190)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>oracle_ebs_ibytransmit,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- POST method to ibytransmit: elevated suspicion.

       CVE-2026-46817 is exploited exclusively via HTTP POST. The vulnerability
       allows the POST body payload to invoke an internal Oracle Java function
       without authentication. A POST to ibytransmit from any non-whitelisted
       source, or from any external IP, should trigger investigation. Authorized
       Oracle Payments trading partner transmissions typically originate from
       known, allowlisted IP ranges on a defined schedule.

       GET and HEAD requests to ibytransmit may occur during connectivity probes
       by legitimate monitoring systems; POST is the specific exploit delivery
       method and carries a higher suspicion level. -->
  <rule id="103966" level="11">
    <if_sid>103965</if_sid>
    <field name="RequestMethod">POST</field>
    <description>Oracle EBS CVE-2026-46817: POST to Oracle Payments ibytransmit from $(ClientHost) at $(RequestHost) -- unauthenticated POST to File Transmission interface is the CVE-2026-46817 exploit delivery method; review source IP against authorized partner allowlist (T1190)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>oracle_ebs_ibytransmit,post_method,cve_2026_46817,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- POST to ibytransmit returning HTTP 2xx: authentication bypass confirmed.

       Under a correctly patched or normally configured Oracle EBS instance,
       POST requests to ibytransmit from unauthenticated or unauthorized
       callers return HTTP 401 (Unauthorized) or 403 (Forbidden). A 2xx
       response status indicates the server accepted and processed the
       unauthenticated request, confirming the missing authentication check
       described in CVE-2026-46817.

       This is a high-confidence signal that active exploitation is occurring.
       The source IP, timestamp, and request body should be captured immediately
       for incident response. -->
  <rule id="103967" level="13">
    <if_sid>103966</if_sid>
    <field name="DownstreamStatus" type="pcre2">^2\d{2}$</field>
    <description>Oracle EBS CVE-2026-46817: POST to Oracle Payments ibytransmit from $(ClientHost) returned HTTP $(DownstreamStatus) -- authentication bypass confirmed at HTTP layer; server processed unauthenticated POST; active CVE-2026-46817 exploitation; investigate immediately (T1190/T1548)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
      <id>T1548</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>oracle_ebs_ibytransmit,post_method,auth_bypass,cve_2026_46817,high_confidence,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- Request to ibytransmit with file path or LFI payload patterns.

       The CVE-2026-46817 exploit payload calls an internal Oracle Java function
       and redirects it to read arbitrary files from the EBS server. Confirmed
       targets from in-the-wild exploitation include /etc/passwd. High-value
       attacker follow-on targets include:
         - Oracle DBC configuration files (.dbc): contain JDBC connection
           strings and may include plaintext database passwords
         - Oracle wallet files (.p12, .sso, cwallet.sso): encrypted credential
           stores for Oracle advanced security
         - appsweb.cfg, wdbc.cfg, admin.xml: Oracle EBS application config
           files with database connection and authentication details
         - /etc/shadow: system password hashes
         - Path traversal sequences (../../) to reach files outside web root

       Presence of any of these patterns in the raw request body indicates the
       attacker is attempting file-read exploitation via the Java function
       redirection attack chain described in CVE-2026-46817. -->
  <rule id="103968" level="14">
    <if_sid>103965</if_sid>
    <field name="rawRequest" type="pcre2">(?i)(?:/etc/passwd|/etc/shadow|/etc/oracle|/etc/oratab|\.\.\/|%2e%2e[%2f/]|%252e%252e|/opt/oracle|/u01/oracle|/u02/oracle|oracle\.dbc|\.dbc\b|cwallet\.sso|ewallet\.p12|\.ora\b|wdbc\.cfg|appsweb\.cfg|admin\.xml|dbc_file|/oracle/product|/app/oracle)</field>
    <description>Oracle EBS CVE-2026-46817 LFI payload: Request to ibytransmit from $(ClientHost) at $(RequestHost) contains file path or path traversal pattern -- attacker invoking internal Oracle Java function to read credential or configuration files via CVE-2026-46817; likely targeting Oracle DBC/wallet files or system credentials (T1190/T1083/T1552.001)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
      <id>T1083</id>
      <id>T1552.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>oracle_ebs_ibytransmit,lfi,file_read,cve_2026_46817,high_confidence,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- POST 2xx to ibytransmit with file path payload: confirmed exploitation.

       This rule combines the two highest-confidence signals:
         1. POST method (the exploit delivery vector)
         2. 2xx response status (auth bypass confirmed)
         3. File path / LFI pattern in the request body (file read payload present)

       All three conditions together constitute confirmed active exploitation of
       CVE-2026-46817. An unauthenticated POST that was accepted (2xx) and that
       contains a file path payload demonstrates the full attack chain is executing.
       Incident response should be initiated immediately: isolate the EBS server
       from the network, capture the full request including body, and audit
       all files that may have been exfiltrated. -->
  <rule id="103969" level="15">
    <if_sid>103966</if_sid>
    <field name="DownstreamStatus" type="pcre2">^2\d{2}$</field>
    <field name="rawRequest" type="pcre2">(?i)(?:/etc/passwd|/etc/shadow|/etc/oracle|/etc/oratab|\.\.\/|%2e%2e[%2f/]|%252e%252e|/opt/oracle|/u01/oracle|/u02/oracle|oracle\.dbc|\.dbc\b|cwallet\.sso|ewallet\.p12|\.ora\b|wdbc\.cfg|appsweb\.cfg|admin\.xml|dbc_file|/oracle/product|/app/oracle)</field>
    <description>Oracle EBS CVE-2026-46817 EXPLOITATION CONFIRMED: POST to Oracle Payments ibytransmit from $(ClientHost) returned $(DownstreamStatus) with file read payload -- unauthenticated Java function invocation succeeded; attacker likely reading Oracle credential or configuration files from server filesystem; IMMEDIATE INCIDENT RESPONSE REQUIRED (T1190/T1083/T1552.001/T1548)</description>
    <options>alert_by_email</options>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
      <id>T1083</id>
      <id>T1552.001</id>
      <id>T1548</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>oracle_ebs_ibytransmit,lfi,file_read,auth_bypass,cve_2026_46817,critical,high_confidence,cisa_kev,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- Rapid probe burst to ibytransmit from a single source.

       Automated exploit frameworks and vulnerability scanners issue rapid
       sequential requests to the ibytransmit endpoint when scanning for
       CVE-2026-46817. A burst of 8 or more requests from the same source
       IP within a 60-second window against this endpoint is consistent
       with automated exploit tool activity: scanner reconnaissance, exploit
       framework enumeration, or repeated exploit attempt iteration.

       Legitimate Oracle Payments integrations operate on scheduled transmission
       windows and do not generate high-frequency request bursts. Any burst
       pattern from an unrecognized IP should be treated as automated exploitation
       tooling. -->
  <rule id="103970" level="9" frequency="8" timeframe="60">
    <if_matched_sid>103965</if_matched_sid>
    <same_field>ClientHost</same_field>
    <description>Oracle EBS CVE-2026-46817: Rapid probe burst from $(ClientHost) to Oracle Payments ibytransmit at $(RequestHost) -- 8+ requests in 60 seconds from same source; automated exploit scanner or CVE-2026-46817 exploitation tooling fingerprint; investigate source IP (T1190)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>oracle_ebs_ibytransmit,probe_burst,scanner,cve_2026_46817,</group>
  </rule>


  <!-- ===========================================================
       Layer 2: Oracle HTTP Server / Apache access log (non-Traefik)
       Rule ID: 103971

       For environments where Oracle EBS traffic is not routed through a
       Traefik reverse proxy, Oracle HTTP Server (OHS) - which is based on
       Apache HTTP Server - generates access logs in combined log format.
       These logs are typically forwarded to Wazuh via the Wazuh agent file
       ingestion or syslog. This rule matches the raw combined-format log
       line for a POST to ibytransmit with a 2xx response code.

       Oracle HTTP Server access log format (combined):
         client_ip - user [timestamp] "METHOD /path HTTP/version" STATUS bytes
       Example:
         198.51.100.42 - - [15/Jul/2026:03:14:01 -0500] "POST /OA_HTML/ibytransmit HTTP/1.1" 200 4821
       =========================================================== -->

  <!-- Apache/OHS access log: POST to ibytransmit with 2xx response.

       Covers non-Traefik environments where Oracle HTTP Server access logs
       are forwarded directly to Wazuh. Matches combined-format log lines
       containing the POST method, the ibytransmit path, and a 2xx status
       code. This is the same exploitation signal as rule 103967 but detected
       from the application server access log rather than a reverse proxy.

       Expected source in non-exploit traffic: known Oracle Payments partner
       integration IPs on scheduled transmission windows. Any source IP that
       is not in the authorized partner allowlist, or any request outside
       the scheduled transmission window, should be treated as an exploitation
       attempt. -->
  <rule id="103971" level="12">
    <match type="pcre2">(?i)POST\s+/OA_HTML/ibytransmit\s+HTTP/[\d.]+"\s+2\d{2}\s</match>
    <description>Oracle EBS CVE-2026-46817 OHS/Apache log: POST to Oracle Payments ibytransmit returned 2xx on $(agent.name) -- authentication bypass confirmed in Oracle HTTP Server or Apache access log; active CVE-2026-46817 exploitation; review source IP and request body for file-read payload (T1190/T1548)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
      <id>T1548</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>oracle_ebs_ibytransmit,post_method,auth_bypass,cve_2026_46817,apache_log,</group>
  </rule>

</group>
